    "enlarge": {
      "word": "ENLARGE",
      "definitions": [
        {
          "text": "To make larger; to increase in quantity or dimensions; to extend in limits; to magnify; as, the body is enlarged by nutrition; to enlarge one's house. To enlarge their possessions of land. Locke.",
          "partOfSpeech": "verb"
        },
        {
          "text": "To increase the capacity of; to expand; to give free scope or greater scope to; also, to dilate, as with joy, affection, and the like; as, knowledge enlarges the mind. O ye Corinthians, our . . . heart is enlarged. 2 Cor. vi. 11.",
          "partOfSpeech": "verb"
        },
        {
          "text": "To set at large or set free. [Archaic] It will enlarge us from all restraints. Barrow. Enlarging hammer, a hammer with a slightly rounded face of large diameter; -- used by gold beaters. Knight. complying with it. Abbott. discourse. \"They enlarged themselves on this subject.\" Clarendon. See Increase.",
          "partOfSpeech": "verb"
        },
        {
          "text": "To grow large or larger; to be further extended; to expand; as, a plant enlarges by growth; an estate enlarges by good management; a volume of air enlarges by rarefaction.",
          "partOfSpeech": "verb"
        },
        {
          "text": "To speak or write at length; to be diffuse in speaking or writing; to expatiate; to dilate. To enlarge upon this theme. M. Arnold.",
          "partOfSpeech": "verb"
        },
        {
          "text": "(Naut.)",
          "partOfSpeech": "verb"
        },
        {
          "text": "To get more astern or parallel with the vessel's course; to draw aft; -- said of the wind.",
          "partOfSpeech": "verb"
        }
      ],
